{"entries":[{"caption":"Conditional average treatment effects (CATEs) of temperature interventions on TSV probabilities by time of day. Posterior mean causal risk differences and 95% credible intervals are shown for TSV categories under hypothetical temperature interventions across exposure duration (1.0, 2.0, and 3.0 h after intervention onset). a) Time-of-day-stratified CATEs for the heating intervention (25.0 °C to 23.5 °C at 35% relative humidity). b) Afternoon–evening differences in CATEs for heating intervention, representing effect modification on the additive (risk-difference) scale. c) Time-of-day-stratified CATEs for the cooling intervention (24.5 °C to 26.0 °C at 55% relative humidity). d) Afternoon–evening differences in CATEs for cooling intervention, representing effect modification on the additive (risk-difference) scale. All estimates are calculated by fixing biological sex to male.","key":"fig-day-specific-CATE","order":{"number":4,"section":[3,3,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"","key":"eq-likelihood","order":{"number":4,"section":[2,4,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"","key":"eq-baseline-category-logit","order":{"number":5,"section":[2,4,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Posterior expected utility-weighted thermal dissatisfaction index (UWTDI) under setpoint interventions. Contours show posterior mean UWTDI under the Fanger weighting scheme as a function of air temperature and relative humidity. Red arrows indicate heating interventions (reductions in heating setpoint), whereas blue arrows indicate cooling interventions (increases in cooling setpoint). Annotated values give the posterior mean UWTDI before and after each intervention for male and female occupants across different exposure durations.","key":"fig-UWTDI-heatmap","order":{"number":6,"section":[3,4,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"","key":"eq-linear-predictor","order":{"number":6,"section":[2,4,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Conditional average treatment effects (CATEs) of temperature interventions on TSV probabilities by sex. Posterior mean causal risk differences and 95% credible intervals are shown for TSV categories under hypothetical temperature interventions across exposure duration (1.0, 2.0, and 3.0 h after intervention onset). a) Sex-stratified CATEs for the heating intervention (25.0 °C to 23.5 °C at 35% relative humidity). b) Male–female differences in CATEs for heating intervention, representing effect modification on the additive (risk-difference) scale. c) Sex-stratified CATEs for the cooling intervention (24.5 °C to 26.0 °C at 55% relative humidity). d) Male–female differences in CATEs for cooling intervention, representing effect modification on the additive (risk-difference) scale. All estimates are calculated by fixing the time of day to afternoon.","key":"fig-sex-specific-CATE","order":{"number":3,"section":[3,2,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"","key":"eq-CATE","order":{"number":1,"section":[2,2,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Utility weights (bounded [0,1])","key":"tbl-utility-weights","order":{"number":2,"section":[2,3,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"","key":"eq-utily-weighted-index","order":{"number":2,"section":[2,3,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"","key":"eq-expected-TSV","order":{"number":7,"section":[3,1,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Causal assumptions","key":"tbl-causal-assumptions","order":{"number":1,"section":[2,2,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Posterior expected TSV across exposure duration and sex. Facet matrices compare posterior expected TSV for males (left) and females (right) across a 3 h exposure period (rows: 1.0 and 3.0 h) as a function of air temperature and relative humidity. Background gradients represent the posterior mean TSV, calculated by fixing the time of day to afternoon. Black contour lines indicate PMV iso-comfort curves calculated assuming ta = tr, va = 0.05, Icl = 0.6, and M = {1.0, 1.1, 1.2}.","key":"fig-expected-TSV","order":{"number":1,"section":[3,1,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"","key":"eq-utily-weighted-index-diff","order":{"number":3,"section":[2,3,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Posterior predictive probabilities of TSV categories across exposure duration. Posterior predictive probabilities (posterior mean and 95% credible intervals) are shown for TSV categories ranging from –3 (‘cold’) to +3 (‘hot’) as a function of exposure duration (1.0, 2.0, and 3.0 h after intervention onset). Results are stratified by biological sex (males, top rows; females, bottom rows) and environmental condition. a) Heating intervention (25.0 °C to 23.5 °C at 35% relative humidity). b) Cooling intervention (24.5 °C to 26.0 °C at 55% relative humidity). All predictions are calculated by fixing the time of day to afternoon and marginalising over varying effects across experimental groups.","key":"fig-sex-specific-prediction","order":{"number":2,"section":[3,2,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Posterior distributions of the utility-weighted thermal dissatisfaction index (UWTDI) under setpoint interventions. Posterior distributions of UWTDI are shown separately for females (violet) and males (orange) at 1.0, 2.0, and 3.0 h after intervention onset. For the heating intervention (top), the thermostat setpoint was reduced from a' (25.0 °C, 35% relative humidity) to a (23.5 °C, 35% relative humidity). For the cooling intervention (bottom), the thermostat setpoint was increased from a' (24.5 °C, 55% relative humidity) to a (26.0 °C, 55% relative humidity). The left and middle columns show the posterior distributions under the baseline (a') and intervention (a) setpoints, respectively, whereas the right column shows the posterior distribution of the counterfactual contrast (a - a'), representing the estimated change in UWTDI attributable to the intervention. Positive values indicate an increase in UWTDI under the intervention relative to baseline, whereas negative values indicate a decrease. Vertical dotted lines indicate the reference value of 20% UWTDI (left and middle columns) and zero difference (right column), while the vertical ticks at the bottom of the plot represent the distribution means.","key":"fig-UWTDI-distribution","order":{"number":7,"section":[3,4,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Posterior utility-weighted thermal dissatisfaction index (UWTDI) under alternative weighting schemes. Posterior UWTDI is shown as a function of air temperature for the Fanger, Comfort, and Symmetric weighting schemes under two relative humidity conditions (30% and 70%) and two exposure durations (1.0 and 3.0 h). Solid coloured lines represent posterior mean UWTDI estimates for females (violet) and males (orange), with shaded bands indicating the corresponding 95% credible intervals. Black dotted, dot-dashed, and dashed lines denote PPD for metabolic rates of 1.0, 1.1, and 1.2 met, respectively. The grey horizontal dotted line marks a UWTDI of 20%, corresponding to the conventional thermal comfort criterion.","key":"fig-UWTDI-temperature","order":{"number":5,"section":[3,4,0,0,0,0,0]}}],"headings":["introduction","methods","dataset","causal-estimand","utility-weighted-outcome-index","statistical-analysis","results","pmv-based-thermal-sensation-prediction-performance","sex-specific-heterogeneity-in-intervention-responses","time-of-day-heterogeneity-in-intervention-responses","decision-oriented-thermal-comfort-evaluation","discussion","limitations","conclusion","acknowledgements","references","supplementary-material","supporting-analyses","data-and-code-availability"],"options":{"eq-prefix":"Eq.","fig-prefix":"Fig.","fig-title":"Fig.","title-delim":"."}}